For small signals, \(V_{DD}\), \(V_{b1}\), and \(V_{b2}\) are AC grounds. Since both the gate and source of \(M_2\) are fixed, \(M_2\) appears simply as \(r_{o2}\) between node \(X\) and AC ground.

Thus, \(M_1\) has source degeneration \(r_{o2}\), giving

\[
\boxed{R_{\text{out}}
=r_{o1}+r_{o2}+g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
=r_{o1}+\left(1+g_{m1}r_{o1}\right)r_{o2}}
\]

where body effect has been neglected. Usually \(g_{m1}r_{o1}\gg 1\), so

\[
\boxed{R_{\text{out}}\approx g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}}
\]

If the body effect of \(M_1\) is included, replace \(g_{m1}\) by \(g_{m1}+g_{mb1}\):

\[
R_{\text{out}}
=r_{o1}+r_{o2}+(g_{m1}+g_{mb1})r_{o1}r_{o2}.
\]

For small signals, \(V_{DD}\), \(V_{b1}\), and \(V_{b2}\) are AC grounds. Since both the gate and source of \(M_2\) are fixed, \(M_2\) presents only \(r_{o2}\) from node \(X\) to AC ground. Thus, \(M_1\) is source-degenerated by \(R_S=r_{o2}\).

Neglecting body effect, the output resistance is

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}
=r_{o1}+r_{o2}+g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
=r_{o1}+\left(1+g_{m1}r_{o1}\right)r_{o2}
}
\]

and, for \(g_{m1}r_{o1}\gg 1\),

\[
\boxed{R_{\text{out}}\approx g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}.}
\]

If the body effect of \(M_1\) is included, replace \(g_{m1}\) by \(g_{m1}+g_{mb1}\):

\[
R_{\text{out}}
=r_{o1}+r_{o2}+(g_{m1}+g_{mb1})r_{o1}r_{o2}.
\]
